+# Contract: Keel proportionality table — config routing (001-bench)
+
+**Feature**: `specs/001-bench/spec.md` · **Date**: 2026-10-26
+
+The "interface" of this documentation-only feature is the proportionality table as published
+readers consume it: a mapping from *the change* to *what the PR body must carry*. This contract
+fixes the target state of that interface so both canonical locations are edited to the same
+normative form, and so `quickstart.md` has something checkable against.
+
+**Canonical locations (exhaustive)**:
+1. `docs/process/DELIVERY.md` — section 2, table headed "The change | What goes in the PR body".
+2. `AGENTS.md` — "Keel: how code is written here" section, same table.
+
+`docs/keel/KEEL_SPEC.md` was checked and contains no copy of the table; it is not part of this
+contract and stays untouched.
+
+## 1. Target table rows (what the interface states after the change)
+
+Both locations must present the following routing. Cell text shown is normative for the
+config-related content; cells marked *unchanged* keep their existing wording verbatim, including
+the two files' pre-existing, deliberately preserved asymmetries elsewhere ("Nothing beyond the
+summary." vs "Nothing beyond the task record." — that difference predates this feature and SC-005
+does not require rewriting it).
+
+| # | The change | What the PR body must carry | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Docs, comments, **inert config values**, a one-line fix, a test-only fix | Nothing beyond the summary / task record (unchanged per file) | **Changed** — "config values" becomes "**inert** config values". After this edit the phrase "config values" MUST NOT appear anywhere on this row unqualified (FR-001, SC-001). |
+| 2 | A bounded code change on the direct route (above) | A three-line card: the paths in scope, the promise, the command that proves it | Unchanged. |
+| 3 | **A change to a runtime-affecting or security-affecting config value (for example, a Runtime timeout or a permission setting) — even if the diff is one line** | **A full card, and behavioural evidence that observes the changed behaviour; parsing or shape validation alone does not count** | **New row.** Satisfies FR-003 and FR-005 and carries User Story 2's exact probe cases (timeout, permission) in the table text itself. |
+| 4 | New public surface: a new module, type, public function, dependency or test file | The full card, and the new surface named in the PR body | Unchanged. |
+| 5 | The expanded route: persistence, permissions, compatibility, security, **runtime-affecting config**, external effects | The full card and the JPD flow above | **Changed** — "runtime-affecting config" added to the obligation list by name (FR-004, SC-003). |
+
+Row order: the new row (3) sits between the bounded-code-change row and the new-public-surface row.
+Rationale: the config route is keyed on effect, not on lines changed, and sits conceptually above
+the bounded-code-change row because the asymmetry rule ("a small diff is not a safe diff") means a
+one-line config edit is never judged lighter than a bounded code change.
+
+## 2. Adjacent note (definition of *inert*; required next to the table — FR-007)
+
+Immediately after each table, an identical paragraph in both files:
+
+> *Inert* means the value neither changes observable runtime behaviour nor affects the security
+> posture of accepted input — for example, a display-only default or UI-only layout config. A
+> value that cannot be classified as inert from the text is treated as runtime-affecting.
+> Behavioural evidence is evidence that observes the changed behaviour; parsing or shape validation
+> alone does not satisfy it.
+
+Contract properties:
+
+- C1 (definition co-location): the paragraph exists in BOTH files, adjacent to the table,
+  verbatim-identical between the files.
+- C2 (default rule): the "cannot be classified → runtime-affecting" clause is part of the note in
+  both files (FR-007; spec Edge Case "reader confusion").
+- C3 (evidence semantics): the note states that behavioural evidence observes behaviour and that
+  parsing/shape validation alone is insufficient (FR-005), reusing the repository's existing JPD
+  formulation rather than inventing a competing one.
+- C4 (language): all new prose is English (AGENTS.md documentation-language rule).
+
+## 3. Consistency obligation between the two locations
+
+- K1: The three config-related obligations — (a) light row limited to *inert*, (b) full card +
+  behavioural evidence for runtime-/security-affecting, (c) runtime-affecting listed under the
+  expanded row — must be present in both files. "Identical routing" (SC-005, US3) is judged by
+  these obligations, so a reader reaching either file first derives the same route for any config
+  edit.
+- K2: No file may imply a lighter route than the other for any of the four SC-004 probes.
+- K3: Neither file may gain a third copy of the table or a paraphrased mini-table; the two
+  canonical locations remain the only published copies.
+
+## 4. Negations (what this contract deliberately does NOT change — FR-006, SC-006)
+
+- N1: No automated checker of the table is introduced (no CI script, no lint rule, no doc-test).
+- N2: `extensions/builtin/graphhelm-development-contracts/policies/keel.yaml` is byte-identical
+  after delivery: no new rule id, no field, no `version` bump (currently `1.1.0`; current ids:
+  `keel.card.*`, `keel.surface.*`, `keel.body.oversized_change`, `keel.ladder`).
+- N3: No gate registration and no workflow file changes.
+- N4: No source file outside `AGENTS.md` and `docs/process/DELIVERY.md` is modified by this
+  feature (excluding `specs/001-bench/` planning artifacts, which are process records, not the
+  delivered docs).
