+# Data Model: Keel config-risk row refinement (001-bench)
+
+**Feature**: `specs/001-bench/spec.md` · **Date**: 2026-10-26
+
+This feature is documentation-only. There is no database, API payload, or new stored object; the
+"entities" below are the *concepts* the prose must let a reader manipulate without ambiguity, as
+defined by the spec's Key Entities section. No fields beyond those listed are introduced.
+
+## Entity 1: Config value — inert
+
+| Attribute | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Definition (published adjacent to both tables) | A configuration value whose one-line change **neither changes observable runtime behaviour nor affects the security posture of accepted input**. |
+| Route | Lightest row → "Nothing beyond the summary" (DELIVERY.md) / "Nothing beyond the task record." (AGENTS.md); in both files the light row now lists it as *inert* config values. |
+| Card weight | Nothing beyond the summary/task record. No card, no behavioural evidence. |
+| Examples named by the spec | A display-only default; a non-behavioural metadata value; UI-only layout config (explicitly remains inert). |
+
+**Validation rules** (how a reader classifies):
+
+- R1: Inertness is judged by what the change *can reach* at runtime or in security posture, not
+  by diff size (a one-line change can be non-inert).
+- R2: A value that cannot be shown inert *from the text* defaults to runtime-affecting. Doubt
+  resolves toward the heavier route (repository asymmetry principle: risk is read from what the
+  change touches, not how much it adds).
+
+## Entity 2: Config value — runtime- or security-affecting
+
+| Attribute | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Definition (published adjacent to both tables) | A configuration value whose change **alters observable runtime behaviour** (e.g., a Runtime timeout) or **alters the security posture** (e.g., a permission setting) of accepted input. |
+| Route | Full card **and** behavioural evidence. Not the light row; not the three-line bounded-change card alone. Also listed among the expanded-row obligations in both tables. |
+| Card weight | Full card (the four-move card: promise, paths, the command that proves each), plus behavioural evidence on top. |
+| Examples named by the spec | A one-line Runtime timeout edit; a one-line permission-setting edit; a concurrency/retry limit (looks inert, changes behaviour at scale → runtime-affecting); a toggle that exposes or hides sensitive data (small runtime footprint, still security-affecting). |
+
+**Validation rules**:
+
+- R3: Behavioural evidence = evidence that observes the changed behaviour. Parsing or shape
+  validation alone does not satisfy it (mirrors the existing JPD proof rule already in AGENTS.md:
+  "Runtime-affecting configuration needs behavioral evidence; parsing or shape validation alone is
+  not proof").
+- R4: A mixed change containing both an inert value and a runtime-/security-affecting value is
+  routed by its heaviest part — the heaviest applicable row wins for the whole change.
+
+## Entity 3: Keel proportionality table (published artifact, two canonical locations)
+
+| Attribute | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Locations (exhaustive — verified by full-repo search) | `docs/process/DELIVERY.md`, section 2 ("What the PR body carries"); `AGENTS.md`, "Keel: how code is written here" section. `docs/keel/KEEL_SPEC.md` contains no copy and stays untouched. |
+| Structure after the change | Same four-row shape plus: (a) the light row's config mention qualified as *inert*; (b) a new/dedicated row stating the full-card + behavioural-evidence route for runtime- or security-affecting config; (c) "runtime-affecting config" added to the expanded row's obligation list. Exact target wording is the contract in `contracts/`. |
+| Invariants (what consistency means operationally) | SC-001: zero unqualified "config values" occurrences on the light row of either file. SC-002/SC-003: both files (2/2) carry the full-card + behavioural-evidence statement and the expanded-row listing. SC-005: identical *config-related routing*; pre-existing asymmetries in unrelated cells (e.g., "task record" vs "summary") are preserved, not rewritten by this feature. |
+| Forbidden changes (FR-006) | No automated checker added; `policies/keel.yaml` untouched (no new rule id, no version bump — current rules: `keel.card.*`, `keel.surface.*`, `keel.body.*`, `keel.ladder`); no gate registration. |
+
+## Relationships
+
+```text
+config value ──classified as──▶ { inert │ runtime-affecting │ security-affecting }
+                                      │                 │                 │
+                                      ▼                 ▼                 ▼
+                              light row            full card +      full card +
+                            (summary only)        behavioural        behavioural
+                                                  evidence           evidence
+                                                  + listed under     + listed under
+                                                    expanded row       expanded row
+```
+
+One config edit → exactly one route; if one change touches values of different classes, the
+heaviest class governs (R4). There is no state machine: classification is a pure, repeatable read
+of the value's effect, and the default on doubt is the heavier class (R2).
+
+## Out of scope (entities deliberately not created)
+
+- No `keel.yaml` field or classifier input formalising "inert".
+- No stored per-change classification record; the classification lives in the PR body/card that the
+  route demands, same as today's rows.
+- No new entity for "JPD flow"; it keeps its existing trigger conditions unchanged.
