+# Quickstart: validating the Keel config-risk row refinement (001-bench)
+
+**Feature**: `specs/001-bench/spec.md` · **Date**: 2026-10-26
+
+This is the validation guide, not an implementation guide. It proves the delivered docs work
+end-to-end: a reader who has never seen the codebase can route every probe case correctly from the
+table prose alone, in both published locations, with no code opened and no new tooling.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- The edited files in place (the only two deliverable files):
+  - `AGENTS.md` (Keel section table + adjacent *inert* note)
+  - `docs/process/DELIVERY.md` (section 2 table + adjacent *inert* note)
+- The target wording is fixed by [`contracts/table-contract.md`](contracts/table-contract.md);
+  classifications follow [`data-model.md`](data-model.md). No toolchain install is required for any
+  check below (PowerShell or bash on this checkout both work).
+
+## Scenario A — No unqualified "config values" survive on the light row (FR-001, SC-001)
+
+Run from the repository root:
+
+```powershell
+Select-String -Path AGENTS.md,docs/process/DELIVERY.md -Pattern 'Docs, comments,'
+```
+
+**Expected**: exactly two matches (one per file), each reading `Docs, comments, **inert** config
+values, ...` — i.e., every light-row mention of config values is qualified as *inert*, in both
+files. Any match showing unqualified `config values` on that line fails the scenario.
+
+```powershell
+git diff -- AGENTS.md docs/process/DELIVERY.md | Select-String -Pattern '^\+.*config'
+```
+
+**Expected**: every added line that mentions config either qualifies it (`inert`,
+`runtime-affecting`, `security-affecting`) or is the expanded-row addition naming
+`runtime-affecting config`.
+
+## Scenario B — Both tables carry identical config-related obligations (FR-002/003/004, SC-002/003/005)
+
+Read side by side:
+
+```powershell
+Get-Content AGENTS.md            # jump to "Keel: how code is written here"
+Get-Content docs/process/DELIVERY.md   # jump to the section 2 table
+```
+
+Checklist per file — all five must hold in BOTH files (K1/K2 of the contract):
+
+1. Light row lists only **inert** config values (Scenario A).
+2. A row states: runtime- or security-affecting config → **a full card AND behavioural evidence**,
+   even when the diff is one line.
+3. The expanded-route row's obligation list includes **runtime-affecting config**.
+4. An adjacent paragraph defines *inert* and states the default-to-runtime-affecting rule (C1/C2).
+5. The evidence semantics sentence (parsing/shape validation alone insufficient) is present (C3).
+
+Then compare the two files' five answers to each other: no file may state a lighter route than the
+other for inert, runtime-affecting, or security-affecting config (SC-005). Pre-existing wording
+differences outside these five items (e.g., "summary" vs "task record") are out of scope and must
+be left untouched.
+
+## Scenario C — The four SC-004 probe cases, routed from table prose alone
+
+Without opening code, pick up each probe and read which row claims it:
+
+| Probe | Correct row / route | Why (as the prose says it) |
+|---|---|---|
+| A display-only default | Light row → nothing beyond the summary | Named in the adjacent note as an *inert* example. |
+| A concurrency/retry limit | Full card + behavioural evidence row (and expanded-row list) | Not showably inert from the text → default rule treats it as runtime-affecting (edge case: looks inert, changes behaviour at scale). |
+| A one-line Runtime timeout edit | Full card + behavioural evidence row | Named verbatim in the new row; one-line diff does not lighten the route. |
+| A one-line permission-setting edit | Full card + behavioural evidence row | Named verbatim in the new row as security-affecting. |
+
+**Pass condition**: all four land where the table says without guessing; the two non-inert probes
+land on full-card-plus-behavioural-evidence, not on the light or three-line-card rows. If a probe
+lands ambiguously, the prose fails SC-004 even if Scenarios A–B pass.
+
+## Scenario D — Docs-only: nothing enforced was touched (FR-006, SC-006)
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```powershell
+git diff --stat
+```
+
+**Expected**: the only modified paths are `AGENTS.md`, `docs/process/DELIVERY.md`, and (as process
+records) the `specs/001-bench/` planning artifacts. Specifically:
+
+- `extensions/builtin/graphhelm-development-contracts/policies/keel.yaml` — **not** in the diff
+  (N2: no new rule id, no version bump from `1.1.0`).
+- No new file under `ci/`, no new script or checker anywhere (N1/N3).
+- No `.github/workflows/` change (N3).
+
+## Scenario E — Reader test (SC-004 end-to-end)
+
+Hand `AGENTS.md` or `docs/process/DELIVERY.md` (either one, chosen independently of the other) to
+a reader who has not seen this spec. Ask them to classify the four Scenario-C probes and to quote
+which sentence did the classifying. **Pass condition**: all four correct, both probes land on full
+card + behavioural evidence, and the cited sentences exist in whichever file they were given —
+proof the rule travels in both published copies, not just one.
+
+## Out of scope for validation
+
+- There is deliberately **no automated test or CI job** for this change (FR-006). Do not add one to
+  make a scenario pass.
+- Repository builds/tests are unaffected: no Rust source changes. Running `cargo test` is not a
+  required or meaningful observer here beyond confirming nothing else moved, and this quickstart
+  does not depend on it.
